WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.240
- You're going to learn all about
what executive presence is

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:06.030
and how you can have more of it yourself.

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:08.760
And to do that, we are going to
be talking with a good friend

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:10.290
of mine, Allen Weiner.

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:13.290
He is the author of the new
book called The Highflier

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:16.410
Handbook, that book's all
about how you can be seen

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:18.120
as a leader at work, and then of course,

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:20.040
how you can become one yourself.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:22.530
This is part one in a
three-part series with Alan

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:26.430
that we're doing, unpacking
three pieces of his book.

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:28.140
So, welcome, Allen. My first question

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:29.610
that I'm going to pitch right to you is,

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:33.090
what's the most common
question you get at your firm?

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:35.430
- For sure.

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:39.450
It's how could we develop
more executive presence in all

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:40.830
of the people who work here?

00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:42.810
Certainly the senior executives,

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:46.050
but they're all using the
term executive presence.

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:48.630
- Right. And you've been working
now doing, you're like one

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:51.090
of the original executive coaches

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:52.740
specializing in communication.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:54.660
There's a lot of 'em out there now,

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:58.170
but as the young people
say, you are one of the O.G,

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:00.270
the "original gangster" executive coaches.

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:03.120
That's right. So you've been,
you've been observing Fortune

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.750
500 CEOs for decades,
very C-suite executives.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:09.090
You've been around people
with executive presence.

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:12.210
Some people call it pizazz,
some people call it flare.

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:14.100
Some people call charisma these days.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:15.990
That's a big word today, charisma.

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:17.250
The through line,

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:19.500
through all this is
its executive presence.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:22.440
So in terms of that phrase,
executive presence, what does

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.720
that mean to you in everyday language?

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:28.320
- Well, it actually started
with a second to five qualities

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:29.400
of credibility

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:32.550
and leadership, which has
always been called composure.

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:35.610
So when I first started hearing
the word executive presence,

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:38.910
it just took me back to all
of the early research on

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:41.070
what was called executive composure.

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:45.660
Composure. Like composed
not all over the place,

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:47.010
implies some kind

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:50.640
of control over the way
people express themselves.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.410
- And then that comes across in the book

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:53.910
as you described it, instead of thinking

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:56.340
of executive presence, think of it as

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.650
that executive's presence in the room,

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:01.320
how people feel their presence.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:04.320
- Right. You know, one of
my favorite stories is A CEO

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:07.020
who said to me, people
come into the office,

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:09.750
senior level people, and they talk to me,

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:13.230
and it sounds like a
drum solo in a jazz club.

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:14.280
It's all over the place.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.170
And sometimes they run
up to the whiteboard

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:19.830
and just start drawing
stuff while they're talking.

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:21.630
Can you coach them

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:25.950
to sound more like a
composition like Mozart,

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:28.470
where there's a beginning,
middle, and an end?

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:30.930
That is what most of people

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:34.500
who are looking at this
experience all over the place.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:37.440
- Right? So we see in a person
who's all over the place,

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:39.540
they're lacking that composure.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:42.480
And we do not see that
as executive presence.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:44.910
In contrast, you talk about control

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:48.960
and composure in terms of
how they present themselves.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:52.590
And you mentioned, for
example, movies The Godfather.

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:54.960
Can you talk about more
what that self-control?

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:56.880
Because when we say control,
a lot of people think, oh,

00:02:56.880 --> 00:02:58.620
you mean control other people?

00:02:58.620 --> 00:02:59.920
But that's not what you're talking about.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:02.800
You're talking about their
ability to stay in control

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:04.930
of themselves and to stay composed.

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:06.190
And you mentioned The Godfather,

00:03:06.190 --> 00:03:07.540
which I think is a great example.

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:11.500
- Well, what happens there
is a, there's a scene

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:14.080
and Marlon Brando, who
plays the Godfather,

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:18.940
does not move a muscle while
another person coming to him

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:21.130
for a favor is asking for it.

00:03:21.130 --> 00:03:23.830
So he sits generally like this

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:29.290
doesn't even nod his head.

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:33.370
And at the end of that
scene, he puts a cat down

00:03:33.370 --> 00:03:35.590
and then finally talks a little himself.

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:37.210
And when he does that,

00:03:37.210 --> 00:03:39.430
there's an amazing
amount of control there.

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:42.400
He says something like,
you come to me asking

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:44.530
for justice pause.

00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:48.970
- Mm. - Yet you've never
invited me to your home.

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:54.070
Pause. So that means someone
exuding that same level

00:03:54.070 --> 00:03:56.140
of control in a meeting.

00:03:56.140 --> 00:04:00.520
Mike say something like,
that's what we're here for.

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:05.520
I want to hear more about
that. Why don't we get started?

00:04:05.755 --> 00:04:08.080
It, it has that kind of quality to it.

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:12.490
- Very crisp communication, very composed

00:04:12.490 --> 00:04:14.410
presentation of themselves.

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:17.590
They're not flailing around,
they're not doing a drum solo.

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:18.880
Interestingly, it's not a drum solo.

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:21.610
It's composed just like a, you know, just

00:04:21.610 --> 00:04:23.500
- Musical piece. Just like
a composition piece. Right.

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:25.720
- You also talk about gravitas in the book

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:28.030
and how people often
ask, Hey, we want to speak

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:29.650
with more gravitas.

00:04:29.650 --> 00:04:30.700
So what do they mean by that?

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:32.920
And how would you coach
people to have more gravitas?

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:36.070
- Well, gravitas has become
yet another word that's used

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:38.110
to describe executive composure.

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:41.470
Sometimes the call is, can
you help develop gravitas?

00:04:41.470 --> 00:04:44.920
Well, as the word implies,
there's a certain gravity

00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:48.040
to the way people express themselves.

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:51.880
The simplest example of that is in speed.

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:55.900
There's an example in my first
book where a president says,

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:58.840
Americans have a right to safety in

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:01.330
and around their homes.

00:05:02.230 --> 00:05:07.230
That gravity of bringing the
tone of voice down, a tone as

00:05:07.660 --> 00:05:12.040
he gets to the end, that's
what gravitas means.

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:13.810
Weightiness.

00:05:13.810 --> 00:05:18.010
Now, hopefully the content
itself has weightiness,

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:21.310
but as I always tell clients,
we're a songwriting team.

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:23.650
I don't write the lyrics to the songs.

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:28.210
So I hope that your
content has gravity to it.

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:31.240
But as far as the description
of it, how you say it,

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:33.460
it's going to be a little slower

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:35.530
and the tones will be a little deeper.

00:05:35.530 --> 00:05:38.650
- Yeah. And many times the
clients we work with have a lot

00:05:38.650 --> 00:05:40.240
to say they have substance,

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:44.500
but their drum solo is
getting in the way of

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:47.290
that substance being
heard in a really clear

00:05:47.290 --> 00:05:48.820
and concise way.

00:05:48.820 --> 00:05:50.410
So what's your advice about Q&amp;A?

00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:54.340
I think at the end of the
chapter about executive presence,

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:56.770
there's a piece about
how to handle q and a,

00:05:56.770 --> 00:05:59.360
because that's where a
lot of this self-control

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:00.680
and composure comes together,

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:03.740
because those are pressurized
moments for some people.

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:06.110
So what's your advice about q and A?

00:06:06.110 --> 00:06:08.300
- This piece of advice about q

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:11.780
and a is one of the ones
I am most happy about.

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:15.470
This is something that I came
onto at the very beginning

00:06:15.470 --> 00:06:16.760
of my practice.

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:18.740
All of the questions that all

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:23.060
of us get throughout our
lives are very, very similar

00:06:23.060 --> 00:06:26.210
to the questions we were
asked on high school testing

00:06:26.210 --> 00:06:27.830
and college testing.

00:06:27.830 --> 00:06:31.550
And they were called true,
false, multiple choice,

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:33.680
fill in the blank and essay.

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:36.530
At least that's the way I experienced it.

00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:38.060
So I tell my clients,

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:41.060
if you are asked a question in a meeting

00:06:41.060 --> 00:06:42.710
that is seeking a yes

00:06:42.710 --> 00:06:47.120
or a no, no matter how
complex the topic is,

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:49.880
it's still a true false question.

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:53.510
So if, for instance, Alex,
you asked me, do you think

00:06:53.510 --> 00:06:56.450
that if we do it that
way it's going to work

00:06:56.450 --> 00:06:59.420
and I have all of these
complex thoughts in my mind,

00:06:59.420 --> 00:07:02.810
I still have to say to
myself, it's a true, false

00:07:02.810 --> 00:07:04.670
or yes no question.

00:07:04.670 --> 00:07:07.520
In other words, you could have
sent me an email that said,

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:11.690
Alan, you think this is
going to work true or false?

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:13.850
Circle T or F, right?

00:07:13.850 --> 00:07:16.400
I think some people
probably ought to do that.

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:19.550
And multiple choice
questions have options.

00:07:19.550 --> 00:07:22.670
So I'm telling my clients,
when you hear the options,

00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:26.840
just say to yourself, is it A, B, or C?

00:07:26.840 --> 00:07:28.970
You're lucky that they
gave you options when

00:07:28.970 --> 00:07:30.320
they ask the question.

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:32.300
And then there's fill
in the blank questions,

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:36.020
which are always the ones where
you may not know the answer.

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:38.900
How much did we spend three quarters ago?

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:41.630
How much do you think
we'll spend on the same

00:07:41.630 --> 00:07:43.100
thing next year?

00:07:43.100 --> 00:07:44.450
When do you think you'll have it?

00:07:44.450 --> 00:07:47.390
All those questions are fill in the blank.

00:07:47.390 --> 00:07:50.300
And then finally, there are
essay questions, which are the,

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:53.480
what we've always come to
call open-ended questions.

00:07:53.480 --> 00:07:55.670
They all start with why and how.

00:07:55.670 --> 00:07:59.570
So I think that's how my
clients, how all of us ought

00:07:59.570 --> 00:08:02.690
to think about handling q
and A. Does that make sense?

00:08:02.690 --> 00:08:03.770
- Oh, it makes complete sense.

00:08:03.770 --> 00:08:05.720
And you know, I've, I've
heard this from you before.

00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:09.350
'cause I used to work with
you, and this is brilliant,

00:08:09.350 --> 00:08:11.870
because so many people don't do this.

00:08:11.870 --> 00:08:14.450
You ask them a very bottom line question

00:08:14.450 --> 00:08:17.240
and they give you the essay answer instead

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:18.650
of giving you the yes no

00:08:18.650 --> 00:08:20.750
or the fill in the blank,
or the multiple choice.

00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:23.090
So you can distinguish yourself simply

00:08:23.090 --> 00:08:26.090
by answering direct questions directly

00:08:26.090 --> 00:08:29.900
and not going on a tangent
for every direct question.

00:08:29.900 --> 00:08:32.120
And then of course, with essay questions,

00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:33.680
sometimes you are asked a bigger question.

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:35.120
Just stay organized.

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:36.680
This is another thing that Alan teaches.

00:08:36.680 --> 00:08:38.480
Give them talk in bullet points, right?

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:41.840
First, second, third,
keep your story organized,

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:43.490
keep it composed.

00:08:43.490 --> 00:08:44.900
Again, this is part one

00:08:44.900 --> 00:08:48.020
of a three-part series
on Allen's book, how

00:08:48.020 --> 00:08:49.070
to Become a Highflier
(Highflier Handbook),

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:50.690
and How You Can Become a Leader At Work.

00:08:50.690 --> 00:08:53.660
We're going to put links to his
book in the description below.

00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:55.850
Please stay tuned for part two

00:08:55.850 --> 00:08:57.350
where we're going to talk about whether

00:08:57.350 --> 00:08:59.930
or not you're a born leader
and what you can do about it.

00:08:59.930 --> 00:09:01.340
Thanks for hanging out with this, Allen.

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:02.660
- Well, thank you, Alex.

